---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import ResearchLandscape from '../components/ResearchLandscape.astro';
import Highlights from '../components/Highlights.astro';
import Publications from '../components/Publications.astro';
import WorkFit from '../components/WorkFit.astro';

const selectedSlugs = ['counterfactual-self-reports', 'decoded-but-unused', 'conformal-market-redesign'];
import { publications } from '../data/publications';
const selectedPubs = selectedSlugs.map(s => publications.find(p => p.slug === s)!);
---
<Base>
  <!-- Hero / About -->
  <section id="about">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <img src="/profile.png" alt="Phongsakon Mark Konrad" class="hero-photo" />
        <div>
          <h1 class="hero-name">Phongsakon <span class="brand-mark">Mark</span> Konrad</h1>
          <p class="hero-tag">do models understand the world, or only imitate it</p>
          <div class="about">
            <p>A sushi chef learning his cuts, except the fish is a model. I take models apart by hand to see what is really happening inside.</p>
            <p>I just graduated with a BSc in Software Engineering from <a href="https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/cis/laboratories/data-and-intelligence-lab">SDU</a>, where I started as a research assistant at the Data and Intelligence Lab and over time grew into leading my own research projects with the group. I also collaborate with the <a href="https://hkust.edu.hk/">HKUST</a> DataVISards group, TA for AI (where I built an <a href="https://phomarkon.github.io/ai101/#/welcome" target="_blank" rel="noopener">interactive AI course</a>), and founded SaturoLabs. This October I begin the <a href="https://www.mlmi.eng.cam.ac.uk/">MPhil in Machine Learning and Machine Intelligence at the University of Cambridge</a>, to build the theoretical foundation for a PhD in this direction. My research has been accepted at venues including ICML workshops, one as an oral, and I release code and model weights wherever I can.</p>
            <p class="rq-heading">What I work on</p>
            <p>For two years I followed my curiosity wherever it led: flood maps from radar, ripeness from a fruit's spectrum, tumours in histology, agents that write software, circuits inside language models. Tasting every fish on the counter was the point. But the same fault line ran through all of it. The models that failed, failed the moment they were only imitating the structure of the problem instead of recovering it.</p>
            <p>So I have chosen my cut. I now work on one question: the foundations of how learning installs representations, and whether those representations capture the world's real causal structure or only imitate it. That puts me at the intersection of representation learning, causal representation learning, and the theory of learning, with <a href="/publications/counterfactual-self-reports">counterfactual reasoning</a> as the test I trust most for telling understanding from pattern matching. I work phenomenon first, building small settings where I know the causal ground truth so I can watch <a href="/publications/decoded-but-unused">what learning actually recovers</a>.</p>
            <div class="rq-questions">
              <p class="rq-q"><span class="rq-qn">Q1</span>When does learning actually identify the causal structure behind the data, and when does it settle for a surface that predicts well but only imitates that structure?</p>
              <p class="rq-q"><span class="rq-qn">Q2</span>When two mechanisms look identical on the training distribution, what inductive bias or intervention makes the difference recoverable, rather than merely assumed?</p>
            </div>
            <p class="rq-fit">I am drawn to the empirical-inference tradition, where identifiability and causal structure are the central questions. The formal machinery, from kernel methods to statistical learning theory, is what I am going to Cambridge to learn. For now I bring the phenomena, the experiments, and a guarantee I can check. I am always glad to talk these through.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-social">
            <a href="mailto:phongsakon@outlook.dk">phongsakon@outlook.dk</a>
            <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=zPHjLREAAAAJ&hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Scholar ↗</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/phomarkon" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
            <a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0004-2521-7879" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ORCID ↗</a>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/phongsakonmarkkonrad" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn ↗</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Selected Work -->
  <section id="selected-work">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Selected Work</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">A few papers that best represent my current research direction.</p>
      <div class="sw-section">
        {selectedPubs.map((p, i) => (
          <a class="sw-card" href={`/publications/${p.slug}`}>
            <span class="sw-num">{i + 1}</span>
            <div>
              <div class="sw-head">
                <span class="sw-title">{p.title}</span>
              </div>
              <div class="sw-authors">{p.authors}</div>
              <div class="sw-meta">
                <span class={`sw-venue-badge ${p.status === 'Under Review' ? 'submitted' : p.status === 'Accepted' ? 'accepted' : ''}`}>{p.status === 'Under Review' ? p.status : `${p.venue} · ${p.status}`}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="sw-tldr">{p.oneLine}</p>
            </div>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Where My Work Fits -->
  <section id="fit">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Questions I'm Chasing</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Three questions I keep circling, all versions of one: does a model recover the world's causal structure, or only fit correlations? I come at them phenomenon first, from inside real models.</p>
      <WorkFit />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Research Landscape -->
  <section id="research">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <h2>Research Landscape</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Three modalities, one through line. Hover a paper to see its neighbours light up. Click to open its page.</p>
      <ResearchLandscape />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Publications -->
  <section id="publications">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Publications</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Peer reviewed and accepted work first, then preprints.</p>
      <Publications />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BSc Thesis -->
  <section id="thesis">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>BSc Thesis</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">My bachelor thesis, with code and model weights released.</p>
      <article class="thesis-card">
        <h3 class="thesis-title">Heimdall: Only the Safe Shall Pass</h3>
        <p class="thesis-sub">A conformal verifier between autonomous bidders and the Nordic balancing market</p>
        <p class="thesis-blurb">With Tim Lukas Adam. Heimdall sits between LLM bidding agents and the grid, and it only lets a bid through if it stays above the operator's loss limit with the coverage they asked for. It holds that guarantee straight through the March 2025 rule change, and every accepted and rejected bid stays auditable.</p>
        <div class="thesis-meta">BSc thesis · conformal prediction · safety verification</div>
        <div class="thesis-links">
          <a href="https://github.com/phomarkon/heimdall-src" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code ↗</a>
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/Phongsakon/heimdall" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model weights ↗</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Highlights -->
  <section id="highlights">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Highlights</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">News, talks, and achievements, most recent first.</p>
      <Highlights />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Career Timeline -->
  <section id="timeline">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <h2>Career Timeline</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Where I've studied and worked, in chronological order.</p>
      <Timeline />
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
